ta-track-label=\"Jennifer M. Elward\" data-author-search=\"Elward, Jennifer M.\">Jennifer M. Elward</a><sup><a href=\"#Aff5\">5</a></sup>, </li><li><a data-test=\"author-name\" data-track=\"click_view_author\" data-track-action=\"open author\" data-track-index=\"18_26\" data-track-context=\"researcher popup with no profile\" href=\"#auth-Adrian-Krzyzanowski-Aff5\" data-author-popup=\"auth-Adrian-Krzyzanowski-Aff5\" data-track-label=\"Adrian Krzyzanowski\" data-author-search=\"Krzyzanowski, Adrian\">Adrian Krzyzanowski</a><sup><a href=\"#Aff5\">5</a></sup>, </li><li><a data-test=\"author-name\" data-track=\"click_view_author\" data-track-action=\"open author\" data-track-index=\"19_26\" data-track-context=\"researcher popup with no profile\" href=\"#auth-Peter-Pog_ny-Aff5\" data-author-popup=\"auth-Peter-Pog_ny-Aff5\" data-track-label=\"Peter Pogány\" data-author-search=\"Pogány, Peter\">Peter Pogány</a><sup><a href=\"#Aff5\">5</a></sup>, </li><li><a data-test=\"author-name\" data-track=\"click_view_author\" data-track-action=\"open author\" data-track-index=\"20_26\" data-track-context=\"researcher popup with no profile\" href=\"#auth-Stephen_D_-Pickett-Aff5\" data-author-popup=\"auth-Stephen_D_-Pickett-Aff5\" data-track-label=\"Stephen D. Pickett\" data-author-search=\"Pickett, Stephen D.\">Stephen D. Pickett</a><sup><a href=\"#Aff5\">5</a></sup>, </li><li><a data-test=\"author-name\" data-track=\"click_view_author\" data-track-action=\"open author\" data-track-index=\"21_26\" data-track-context=\"researcher popup with no profile\" href=\"#auth-Ian_D_-Wall-Aff5\" data-author-popup=\"auth-Ian_D_-Wall-Aff5\" data-track-label=\"Ian D. Wall\" data-author-search=\"Wall, Ian D.\">Ian D. Wall</a><sup><a href=\"#Aff5\">5</a></sup>, </li><li><a data-test=\"author-name\" data-track=\"click_view_author\" data-track-action=\"open author\" data-track-index=\"22_26\" data-track-context=\"researcher popup with no profile\" href=\"#auth-Christopher_M_-Bishop-Aff1\" data-author-popup=\"auth-Christopher_M_-Bishop-Aff1\" data-track-label=\"Christopher M. Bishop\" data-author-search=\"Bishop, Christopher M.\">Christopher M. Bishop</a><sup><a href=\"#Aff1\">1</a></sup>, </li><li><a data-test=\"author-name\" data-track=\"click_view_author\" data-track-action=\"open author\" data-track-index=\"23_26\" data-track-context=\"researcher popup with no profile\" href=\"#auth-Philip_G_-Humphreys-Aff5\" data-author-popup=\"auth-Philip_G_-Humphreys-Aff5\" data-track-label=\"Philip G. Humphreys\" data-author-search=\"Humphreys, Philip G.\">Philip G. Humphreys</a><sup><a href=\"#Aff5\">5</a></sup>, </li><li><a data-test=\"author-name\" data-track=\"click_view_author\" data-track-action=\"open author\" data-track-index=\"24_26\" data-track-context=\"researcher popup with no profile\" href=\"#auth-James_A_-Lumley-Aff5\" data-author-popup=\"auth-James_A_-Lumley-Aff5\" data-track-label=\"James A. Lumley\" data-author-search=\"Lumley, James A.\">James A. Lumley</a><span>&#160;\n            <a href=\"https://orcid.org/0000-0002-6060-889X\"><span>ORCID: </span>orcid.org/0000-0002-6060-889X</a></span><sup><a href=\"#Aff5\">5</a></sup>, </li><li><a data-test=\"author-name\" data-track=\"click_view_author\" data-track-action=\"open author\" data-track-index=\"25_26\" data-track-context=\"researcher popup with no profile\" href=\"#auth-Mario_P_-Wiesenfeldt-Aff6\" data-author-popup=\"auth-Mario_P_-Wiesenfeldt-Aff6\" data-track-label=\"Mario P. Wiesenfeldt\" data-author-search=\"Wiesenfeldt, Mario P.\">Mario P. Wiesenfeldt</a><sup><a href=\"#Aff6\">6</a></sup> &amp; </li><li aria-label=\"Show all 26 authors for this article\" title=\"Show all 26 authors for this article\">…</li><li><a data-test=\"author-name\" data-track=\"click_view_author\" data-track-action=\"open author\" data-track-index=\"26_26\" data-track-context=\"researcher popup with no profile\" href=\"#auth-Marwin_H__S_-Segler-Aff1\" data-author-popup=\"auth-Marwin_H__S_-Segler-Aff1\" data-track-label=\"Marwin H. S. Segler\" data-author-search=\"Segler, Marwin H. S.\" data-corresp-id=\"c3\">Marwin H. S. Segler</a><span>&#160;\n            <a href=\"https://orcid.org/0000-0001-8008-0546\"><span>ORCID: </span>orcid.org/0000-0001-8008-0546</a></span><sup><a href=\"#Aff1\">1</a></sup>&#160;</li></ul>\n\n                        \n\n                        <p data-container-section=\"info\">\n                            <a data-test=\"journal-link\" href=\"/\" data-track=\"click\" data-track-action=\"journal homepage\" data-track-category=\"article body\" data-track-label=\"link\"><i>Nature</i></a>\n                             (<span data-test=\"article-publication-year\">2026</span>) <a href=\"#citeas\" data-track=\"click\" data-track-action=\"cite this article\" data-track-label=\"link\">Cite this article</a>\n                        </p>\n                        \n                            \n                        \n                        \n                        <p>\n                            We’re sharing this article early to provide faster access to peer-reviewed, accepted research. It is citable and carries a permanent DOI. This version is subject to further edits and will be replaced automatically by the final Version of Record. All legal disclaimers apply.\n                        </p>\n                    </header>\n\n                    \n    \n    \n\n    \n    \n\n                    \n                </div>\n\n                <div>\n                    <div lang=\"en\" data-title=\"Abstract\" aria-labelledby=\"Abs1\" id=\"Abs1-section\"><h2 id=\"Abs1\">Abstract</h2><p>Chemical synthesis remains a critical bottleneck in the discovery and manufacture of functional small molecules<sup>1−3</sup>. While AI-assisted synthesis planning has proliferated in recent years, a detailed understanding of its failure modes has not been achieved, and models still struggle with predicting less frequent, yet strategically critical reactions, as well as hallucinated, incorrect predictions misaligned with chemists’ expectations<sup>4−12</sup>. In this work, we analyze the failure modes of current AI models and propose RetroChimera: a frontier retrosynthesis model, built upon two newly developed components with complementary inductive biases, integrated via a novel, learning-based ensembling strategy. Through experiments across several orders of magnitude in data scale, we show RetroChimera outperforms leading baselines, demonstrating robustness outside the training data, as well as the ability to learn from very small numbers of examples per reaction class. Using both pairwise and pointwise setups, we find that organic chemists prefer predictions from RetroChimera over published reference reactions and over other AI models. Finally, we demonstrate zero-shot transfer and fine-tuning on internal datasets from two major pharmaceutical companies, showing robust generalization under distribution shift. Our work demonstrates the viability of deep learning for accurate synthesis prediction in increasingly challenging regimes.</p></div>\n                    \n    \n\n\n                    \n                        \n\n                        \n                            <div>\n                                <p data-test=\"institution-access-preview\">\n                                    This is a preview of subscription content, <a href=\"https://wayf.springernature.com?redirect_uri=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.nature.com%2Farticles%2Fs41586-026-11160-9\" data-track=\"click_institution_login\" data-track-context=\"article body link\" data-track-action=\"institution access preview subscription\" data-track-label=\"link\">access via your institution</a>\n                                </p>\n                            </div>\n                        \n\n                        <h2 id=\"access-options\">Access options</h2>\n                        \n\n                        \n    <P data-test=\"entitlement-box-main\">\n        \n            <a href=\"